this is evan langer reading for reviewer  steven petit kirby has reached his  ultimate form in kirby in the forgotten  land as the culmination of 30 plus years  of exploring the malleable mascot his  latest adventure is the most inventive  and best entry in the franchise history  the move to 3d brings kirby's signature  mechanic to life in a way that wasn't  possible before  this feels like a new beginning for  kirby as it maintains all of the  familiar series trappings while  presenting them in a cohesive  well-rounded adventure that is playfully  imaginative throughout  kirby in the forgotten land follows the  traditional stage based format with  multiple areas and rooms in each area  it's certainly not as open as the  trailers have made it seem it's much  closer to super mario 3d world than  super mario odyssey still the stages  hold far more secrets than might  initially meet the eye actively  encouraging you to poke around to find  hidden rooms paths and areas with  self-contained objectives containing  their own rewards at the end though  forgotten land mostly sticks with the  tried and true world themes we've come  to expect from nintendo platformers the  remnants of civilizations present in the  forgotten land give these locales  mysterious vibes that make them unique  from an abandoned shopping mall an  amusement park to a haunted house and  chile metro station each of forgotten  land's 30 plus stages tell their own  story unlike some other recent kirby  games it almost always feels fresh and  full of new ideas  [Music]  this consistent novel charm is  significantly bolstered by the  meaningful use of kirby's copy mechanic  and the brand new mouthful mode the meme  worthy feature isn't a silly gimmick  it's a delightful and interesting  gameplay mechanic that also happens to  be quite funny kirby can only absorb the  powers of living things so when he tries  to inhale inanimate objects he just  stretches his body around them watching  him swallow other characters and take on  their attributes was a lot to deal with  already but now we have to embrace the  chaos that is kirby inhaling cars  commandeering roller coasters and  revolutionizing the aerodynamics of  paragliding  [Music]  each mouthful ability has its own unique  mechanic and is often used to complete  bonus objectives and rescue hidden  waddle dees  the abilities up the gameplay in  creative ways coaster mouth sees kirby  do his best ratchet rail grind  impression ring mouth can knock out  enemies with powerful gusts or be used  to steer a boat and water balloon mouth  turns kirby into a giant blob who spews  water clearing fiery obstacles and  sludge while laying ways to colossal  obstacles that cannot be taken out with  normal copy abilities meanwhile the  sparsely used light bulb mouth assists  with the pitch black haunted house where  cardboard monsters pop out from the  walls and ghost chase the illuminated  kirby bulb mouthful mode shines but  kirby's signature copy ability is still  front and center and more important than  ever before unlike some other kirby  platformers kirby's various copy  abilities are consistently meaningful  from both a puzzle solving and combat  perspective  there are switches that have to be hit  in quick succession with the cutter by  performing a combo move ice blocks that  require fire to melt fiery crates that  need the cooling power of ice floating  targets for ranged weapons nails for the  hammer etc even some of the platforming  sequences are designed with specific  abilities in mind including large gaps  that can only be crossed with the  tornado ability or surfaces that call  for kirby to glide like a figure skater  with the ice ability the 3d perspective  itself is also beneficial to kirby's  abilities as it makes them versatile and  leads to more creative puzzles  experimentation and open-ended  objectives  [Music]  so  while forgotten land likely won't be a  difficult game for those with some  experience with action platformers it is  the most challenging kirby to date it  has two difficulty settings with the  more challenging wild mode boasting some  tricky boss battles that require you to  think about your strategy when it comes  to copy abilities  that said forgotten land is still  inviting to newcomers thanks to the  forgiving spring breeze mode and the  ability to buy health items or attack or  defense boosts blueprints earned by  beating bosses or solving hidden puzzles  can be turned in to evolve copy  abilities hal laboratory did a great job  gradually ramping up the difficulty  alongside your expected ability upgrades  evolved copy abilities deal greater  damage while having additional features  that can expand their versatility in  combat you also need rare stones to  purchase evolve copy abilities these are  earned through optional side stages on  treasure road fun time trials that  revolve around a specific copy ability  or mouthful mode treasure road stages  are bite-sized compared to main levels  but they are well designed and a lot of  fun which is great considering there are  more of these than main levels  [Music]  [Applause]  the main stages and treasure road levels  add up to create a 10 to 15 hour  platformer depending on how thorough you  want to be  copyability upgrades and item purchases  are made in waddle d-town the game's  central hub that expands as you rescue  more waddle dees the town includes a  coliseum for boss rush gauntlets and a  few mini-games including the motion  sensor tilt and roll that nods back to  the game boy color game tilt and tumble  and reflex base fishing and cooking  games  [Music]  you can also spend coins at the gacha  machine to try and collect all the  figures beware there are a lot of  figures and all they do is sit in a  digital album the activities of waddle  d-town aren't that engaging but they're  essentially just cute little add-ons  rather than focal points  [Music]  for parents with young kids the local  co-op mode is a blast even though one  player has to be the decidedly less cool  bandana waddle d who can't steal  abilities from enemies while he's the  coolest waddle d due to the bandana the  second player doesn't get to partake in  the most interesting aspects of each  stage this is most notable during  mouthful sections as bandana waddle d  has to rely on kirby to do his thing to  move on to the next section it's a  better co-op mode than some other  family-friendly switch exclusives but  the experience playing as waddle d isn't  nearly as dynamic when you're stuck with  one set of attacks while kirby has  dozens  kirby in the forgotten land is one of  those games that's hard to play without  constantly having a silly smile on your  face it's far more than just a cute and  charming platformer with colorful  visuals though this is one of the best  platformers on nintendo switch thanks to  its brilliantly designed stages and a  dynamic arsenal of abilities that  consistently shake up the  moment-to-moment platforming and action  [Music]  bye
 

